we can plug the value of the extent of reaction to calculate the equilibrium concentrations. For the example in hand, we found that the reaction extent was $x$, hence we have that the equilibrium concentrations, which is $K_c$=5.10 at 700K, are:
\[ \frac{x^2}{(0.1-x)\cdot (0.2-x) }=K_c   \]
rearanging we have
\[  (1-\frac{1}{K_c})x^2 -0.3x+0.02=0  \]
Solving to $x$ we arrive to two different solutions $x_1$=0.286M and $x_1$=0.087M. The first answer has no chemical meaning, as we start with 0.1M of \ce{CO} and 0.286M cannot decompose from 0.1M. So we have that the final reaction extent is 0.086M. Now we can go back to our ICE chart and compute the equilibrium concentration of each reactant and product: $[$\ce{CO}$]_{Eq}$=0.013M, $[$\ce{H2O}$]_{Eq}$=0.113M, $[$\ce{CO_2}$]_{Eq}$=0.087M and $[$\ce{H2}$]_{Eq}$=0.087M.

